ckmotif-gallery
================

This is the manifest-driven native component gallery.  The JSON inventory in
../../../docs/modern-gallery-manifest.json is the source of truth.  The build
generates gallery_manifest.h and gallery_sources.h from that inventory and the
annotated gallery_examples.c source blocks; those generated files are never
edited by hand.  The source fragments include public construction, callback,
and teardown code.  `gallery_provenance.json` records the source and manifest
hashes and is verified by `make check`.
The manifest-backed documentation catalogue is generated at
`docs/modern-gallery-components.md`; its anchors and manifest hash are checked
alongside the gallery provenance.

The gallery shell uses only public Xm interfaces.  Theme and DPI controls are
wired to `XmApplyAppearanceProfile` and the public display-scale transaction
APIs. Theme cycles through classic, modern light and modern dark. Scale cycles
through 100%, 150%, 200%, 300% and automatic display sizing. Each activation
reads the current public setting, so command-line overrides and external
changes are respected without a stale local counter. PrintShell
is conditional on PRINTING_SUPPORTED and remains visible as an unavailable
optional capability in a print-disabled build.

For a display-backed native smoke test:

  python3 tests/automated/run-gallery-integration.py

For manifest screenshot scenarios, pass a fresh output directory under the
approved temporary volume:

  python3 tests/automated/run-gallery-screenshots.py --binary ABSOLUTE_BINARY \
    --out /Volumes/S8T/tmp/ckmotif-gallery-screenshots \
    --appearance modern-light --scale 1.5

The screenshot runner starts each component with explicit `--appearance` and
`--scale` values. The gallery parses those values strictly and applies them
through the public `XmSetAppearanceProfile` and
`XmSetDisplayScaleOverride` APIs before constructing the examples; invalid values or
a failed transaction abort the scenario.

Startup geometry follows the effective toolkit scale: the default 1100 by 740
logical-pixel client is bounded to 95 percent of the current desktop work area
(or screen when the window manager supplies no valid EWMH work area). Explicit
Xt `-geometry` dimensions and positions remain authoritative. Width/height
resources are also preserved. Window decorations are owned by the window manager;
the gallery does not assume MWM-specific frame dimensions.

Constrained windows present navigation as a full-width page. Use arrow keys to
choose a leaf, then Enter to return to its example; pointer release also
activates it. Widening restores the user's desktop sidebar preference. The
native `--integration-responsive` mode checks transitions, content bounds,
selection versus activation and preference restoration.

The welcome page reflows its prose to the available viewport. The icon catalogue
uses natural cell sizes and includes all nine built-in dialog symbols. Both pages
scroll vertically when needed. To verify their wide/narrow/restore geometry:

  python3 tests/automated/run-gallery-pages.py --binary ABSOLUTE_BINARY \
    --prefix ABSOLUTE_INSTALL_PREFIX --out FRESH_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY

Use --expect-no-svg for a non-SVG build. This runs the gallery's
--integration-pages mode in classic, modern light and modern dark at three scales.
Each run also changes the palette and scale of the existing icon widgets.
Named image bindings own their cache references and refresh them for the target
widget's colours and scale; classic fallback symbols retain their native aspect
ratio. The checks verify assigned resources as well as the rendered pixels.

The List and ComboBox examples request three visible rows for their three sample
items. Their vertical size follows toolkit row metrics instead of a fixed pixel
height or the default larger list capacity. This is sample content allocation,
not a theme-specific style override.

Live appearance qualification
-----------------------------
`--integration-live` applies light, dark and classic profiles, both densities
and 1x/1.5x/2x scales to each available realized component without replacing
its instance. It checks public state, selection, callback counts and embedded
preview containment. Run with an explicit display through
`tests/automated/run-gallery-integration.py --live`; the full catalogue takes
several minutes. The default integration runner also exercises the visible
Theme and Scale controls and verifies focus/state preservation. These checks
do not establish complete visual/interaction acceptance for every widget.

Example instructions describe the constructed example: passive labels,
indicators and presentation controls invite Theme/Scale comparisons and do not
reserve a callback-feedback row. Interactive examples retain their feedback;
popup examples explain how to open their window. Compact descriptions preserve
that distinction. The component integration matrix checks feedback visibility
while moving between passive and interactive examples.

Field examples use native RowColumn layout for captions, controls and helper
text. Growing NumberBox validation messages participate in the layout; the
integration matrix verifies that they do not overlap explanatory text.

NumberBox feedback subscribes to invalid-input and committed-value callbacks.
It labels the last event explicitly and retains the committed quantity when
tentative input is rejected. Native integration checks rejection and recovery.
